nly one opponent disc (E4). This minimizes the "frontier" of pieces available for the opponent to attack, restricting their mobility. It avoids the strategic risks associated with playing next to the opponent\'s wall (like C5) or extending too far (like D6), and avoids the tactical error of playing to an X-square (B2).\n\nEvaluation: (E, 3) is a quiet, centralizing move that strengthens the player\'s position without giving the opponent easy tactical responses or corner access.\nReason: It maximizes central control and stability while minimizing the opponent\'s mobility options.\nAnswer: (E, 3)'}
